WEBVTT

1
00:00:16.706 --> 00:00:17.787
come take a look at this."

2
00:00:18.907 --> 00:00:21.349
Grandpa was reading the newspaper at his desk.

3
00:00:21.369 --> 00:00:25.332
"There's going to be a Renaissance fair in Buck County.

4
00:00:26.192 --> 00:00:26.973
Do you want to go?"

5
00:00:26.993 --> 00:00:29.554
"What's a Renaissance fair?"

6
00:00:29.915 --> 00:00:31.776
Laurie asked, looking up from her book.

7
00:00:32.704 --> 00:00:39.846
"A Renaissance fair," Grandpa began reading from the newspaper ad, "is a place to see, hear, and taste

8
00:00:40.006 --> 00:00:42.827
life as it was during Elizabethan England."

9
00:00:45.728 --> 00:00:46.608
He looked at Laurie.

10
00:00:46.628 --> 00:00:53.870
"That means the time when Queen Elizabeth was on the throne, from 1558 to 1603.

11
00:00:54.990 --> 00:00:57.311
It was the height of the English Renaissance.

12
00:00:58.431 --> 00:01:01.912
There will probably be a market, performances, and actors."

13
00:01:03.068 --> 00:01:04.649
"What does renaissance mean?"

14
00:01:05.029 --> 00:01:05.810
Laurie asked.

15
00:01:05.830 --> 00:01:09.192
"Aren't you taking French at school?"

16
00:01:09.652 --> 00:01:11.213
Grandpa said teasingly.

17
00:01:11.233 --> 00:01:15.755
"Renaissance means rebirth in French.

18
00:01:16.956 --> 00:01:24.701
We call the period of European history from the fourteenth to the sixteenth century the Renaissance because all

19
00:01:24.721 --> 00:01:27.842
of Europe changed dramatically during this time.

20
00:01:28.903 --> 00:01:33.014
There was a rebirth in art, education, government, and religion.

21
00:01:34.115 --> 00:01:39.139
It was also a time of great discovery in both the sciences and geography.

22
00:01:40.561 --> 00:01:44.504
The Renaissance began in Florence, Italy, in the 1300s.

23
00:01:45.765 --> 00:01:52.611
Florence was a big trading city because it was located neatly between the Middle East, Europe, and Asia.

24
00:01:53.732 --> 00:01:57.776
Many merchants, who used to be part of the lower class, became wealthy.

25
00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:01.101
This was the beginning of the middle class.

26
00:02:02.262 --> 00:02:07.385
They weren't nobles or royalty, and they didn't have any fancy titles to their names, but they had

27
00:02:07.525 --> 00:02:08.666
money to spend freely.

28
00:02:10.467 --> 00:02:13.789
They also had free time to think, read, and enjoy life.

29
00:02:14.890 --> 00:02:19.813
Many scholars think this brought about a renewed interest in art and learning.

30
00:02:20.953 --> 00:02:24.856
The middle class wanted to spend their wealth on art, books, and music.

31
00:02:26.048 --> 00:02:31.910
Wealthy families gave money to artists and asked them to make paintings and sculptures for the city.

32
00:02:33.671 --> 00:02:36.452
Many of these artists are still admired today.

33
00:02:37.533 --> 00:02:44.456
Let's see, I'm sure I have a book around here somewhere Grandpa stood up and examined his bookshelf.

34
00:02:44.476 --> 00:02:50.978
"Aha! Artists of the Italian Renaissance," he read, pulling the title from the shelf.

35
00:02:50.998 --> 00:02:54.560
"I'm sure you'll recognize a lot of stuff in here."

36
00:02:55.780 --> 00:02:57.221
Laurie flipped through the pages.

37
00:02:57.241 --> 00:02:59.562
"The Mona Lisa!"

38
00:02:59.943 --> 00:03:00.763
she exclaimed.

39
00:03:01.924 --> 00:03:07.367
"Right! The Mona Lisa was painted by Leonardo da Vinci in the early 1500s."

40
00:03:08.708 --> 00:03:09.829
Laurie turned the page.

41
00:03:09.849 --> 00:03:12.650
"Oh! He's naked!"

42
00:03:13.031 --> 00:03:13.591
she cried.

43
00:03:13.611 --> 00:03:19.334
"That's Michelangelo's famous sculpture David," Grandpa answered.

44
00:03:19.354 --> 00:03:26.129
"During the Renaissance, artists began learning how to create more realistic images of humans.

45
00:03:27.310 --> 00:03:32.311
They studied the human body carefully, and tried to draw and sculpt what they saw.

46
00:03:32.331 --> 00:03:35.672
It was really a revolution in art.

47
00:03:37.373 --> 00:03:41.934
Education in Europe also developed and changed during the Renaissance.

48
00:03:43.174 --> 00:03:48.156
In the Middle Ages, people learned to read and write so they could study and copy the Bible.

49
00:03:49.196 --> 00:03:53.597
But people of the Renaissance wanted to study books from classical Greece and Rome.

50
00:03:54.078 --> 00:03:56.758
This was called new learning or humanism."

51
00:03:56.778 --> 00:04:00.319
"Humanism?" Laurie echoed.

52
00:04:00.339 --> 00:04:02.040
"That's a funny word.

53
00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:04.361
Were they studying humans?"

54
00:04:05.901 --> 00:04:06.882
"Well, yes!"

55
00:04:07.422 --> 00:04:08.402
Grandpa answered.

56
00:04:09.002 --> 00:04:15.960
"People were curious about what human beings could do with science, art, philosophy—everything!

57
00:04:16.040 --> 00:04:21.325
They believed that humans had skills, and that these skills could be developed to perfection.

58
00:04:22.586 --> 00:04:24.268
What's one of your skills, Laurie?"

59
00:04:24.548 --> 00:04:25.409
Grandpa asked.

60
00:04:25.429 --> 00:04:30.293
"Um, I always get As in reading," Laurie replied.

61
00:04:30.313 --> 00:04:37.739
"So you would have been encouraged to develop that talent by reading classic Greek and Roman books.

62
00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:43.004
Unfortunately this would only have been encouraged if you were a boy.

63
00:04:44.280 --> 00:04:49.362
Girls received just basic education and then learned to cook and sew at home.

64
00:04:50.522 --> 00:04:56.864
Rather than going to a university at age 15, like boys did, girls would probably get married to

65
00:04:56.904 --> 00:04:58.744
an older man and start a family."

66
00:04:58.764 --> 00:05:01.465
"That's not fair."

67
00:05:01.825 --> 00:05:02.705
Laurie frowned.

68
00:05:03.986 --> 00:05:05.006
"No, it's not.

69
00:05:06.026 --> 00:05:11.808
But many things came out of the Renaissance that everybody, including women, could appreciate later.

70
00:05:12.980 --> 00:05:18.823
One of the greatest inventions was the printing press, which was created in Germany in 1440.

71
00:05:19.943 --> 00:05:24.305
Thanks to the printing press, books didn't have to be copied by hand anymore.

72
00:05:25.466 --> 00:05:28.087
So they were cheaper and more people could buy them.

73
00:05:29.628 --> 00:05:35.651
Libraries were built and regular people, not just nobles or clergymen, learned how to read.

74
00:05:36.851 --> 00:05:43.125
The printing press changed European—no, human—society forever!

75
00:05:43.926 --> 00:05:46.366
Can you imagine a world without books, Laurie?"

76
00:05:47.527 --> 00:05:49.447
Laurie shook her head forcefully.

77
00:05:49.467 --> 00:05:51.428
"No way!"

78
00:05:52.588 --> 00:05:54.989
"Many things were invented during the Renaissance.

79
00:05:55.849 --> 00:05:58.750
Have you heard of the Italian Galileo Galilei?

80
00:06:00.290 --> 00:06:05.872
Human eyes saw the surface of the moon for the first time with his telescope.

81
00:06:07.104 --> 00:06:12.947
He was also influential in getting people to accept the idea that the Earth moves around the sun,

82
00:06:13.207 --> 00:06:14.607
and not the other way around."

83
00:06:14.627 --> 00:06:18.149
"Of course the Earth moves around the sun!

84
00:06:18.669 --> 00:06:21.510
Even I know that and I'm only in fourth grade."

85
00:06:22.791 --> 00:06:23.591
Laurie giggled.

86
00:06:23.611 --> 00:06:27.193
"Will we see Galileo's telescope at the fair?"

87
00:06:27.593 --> 00:06:28.173
she asked.

88
00:06:29.734 --> 00:06:34.696
"No. Galileo was Italian and we're going to an English fair, remember?

89
00:06:35.788 --> 00:06:42.271
The Renaissance began in Italy, but later it spread to England, France, Germany, and all of Europe.

90
00:06:43.291 --> 00:06:50.534
We'll probably see actors playing famous people of the English Renaissance, like the writer William Shakespeare.

91
00:06:51.795 --> 00:06:58.077
Or Sir John Harington, inventor of the flushing toilet—another great invention of the Renaissance!

92
00:06:59.218 --> 00:07:03.940
And at the market I bet we'll see barrel makers, blacksmiths, and milliners."

93
00:07:05.692 --> 00:07:10.035
"Millionaires!" Laurie said, surprised.

94
00:07:10.055 --> 00:07:15.418
"No—milliners! They make hats," Grandpa answered with a chuckle.

95
00:07:15.438 --> 00:07:19.680
"Hats and wigs were in fashion back then.

96
00:07:20.060 --> 00:07:24.183
In fact, anyone over age 12 was required to wear a hat.

97
00:07:25.844 --> 00:07:30.807
During the Renaissance, clothing was a symbol of wealth and class—by law.

98
00:07:32.367 --> 00:07:36.723
For example, nobody except the queen was allowed to wear purple.

99
00:07:38.824 --> 00:07:42.647
And peasants weren't allowed to wear pink or gold-colored fabric!"

100
00:07:42.667 --> 00:07:45.049
"That's crazy!"

101
00:07:45.409 --> 00:07:46.210
Laurie cried.

102
00:07:47.070 --> 00:07:48.631
She looked down at her own clothes.

103
00:07:48.651 --> 00:07:51.413
"What are we going to wear to the fair?"

104
00:07:51.433 --> 00:07:55.416
"Well, we could go in our regular clothes.

105
00:07:56.557 --> 00:07:58.899
But it would be fun to get dressed up, wouldn't it?"

106
00:07:59.259 --> 00:08:00.200
Grandpa grinned.

107
00:08:00.916 --> 00:08:05.142
He grabbed a long black feather from his desk and stuck it behind her ear.

108
00:08:06.904 --> 00:08:08.226
Laurie's eyes shined.

109
00:08:08.246 --> 00:08:10.228
"Yeah, it would!

110
00:08:10.809 --> 00:08:11.730
I can't wait."
